  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nAs of 1 AM CDT, storms are staying in western SD and out of our \nforecast area. Temperatures are in the 60s with winds out of the \neast to southeast at around 10 mph. A warm front followed by a cold \nfront will move across the region throughout the day and into the \nevening hours. This warm front will help temperatures be a little \nwarmer today with highs in the 80s to low 90s, warmest across \ncentral SD.\n\nThe cold front will help pop off some thunderstorms this evening \nacross central SD that will move southeast and across the area. \nThere could be a second wave that moves down out of North Dakota \ncloser to midnight Tuesday morning. There is a Slight risk (level 2 \nout of 5) for portions of south central SD and portions of \nnortheastern SD and a Marginal risk (level 1 out of 5) for severe \nstorms across the remainder of the forecast area. The main hazards \nfor these storms will be golf ball size hail and 60-75 mph wind \ngusts. PWAT values are around 1.25\" which is in the 97th NAEFS \npercentile so some brief heavy downpours are possible as well, \nalthough as a secondary threat. Overall chance of storms is 30-40% \nwith the first round along the Missouri River and east to the James \nRiver. With the second round after midnight, chances across far \nnortheastern SD increase to 40-60%. Our next widespread chance for \nstorms will be Thursday evening into Friday as a low coming off the \nRockies pushes out high pressure. Saturday and Sunday evenings could \nbe stormy as well as we return to a more active pattern, but \nconfidence on severe weather is low at this time.\n\nTemperatures after today return to right about normal, with a slight \nincrease towards the end of the period. Winds Tuesday behind the \ncold front could gust up to 35 mph in northern SD. That cold\nfront also looks to bring another round of elevated smoke to \nthe area for Tuesday. There could be a band of higher \nconcentrations over northeast SD, but little to no smoke is \nexpected at the surface.",
